This chapter describes how the library encodes one-parameter unitary groups and their generators,
and proves Stone's theorem. It corresponds to
[Section 10.4](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#sctn:stones-theorem)
of the blueprint, which, like Sections 10.2 and 10.3, follows Hall's
[_Quantum Theory for Mathematicians_](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4614-7116-5). It builds on the
{ref "unbounded"}[unbounded chapter]: a generator is an unbounded operator, and the unitary group
$`e^{itA}` comes from the functional calculus of the unbounded spectral theorem. The chapter also
covers two results on unitary groups that Section 10.6 of the blueprint states for the Haag–Kastler
axioms: the positive-energy condition, which rests on Stone's theorem, and the passage from weak to
strong continuity, which supplies its hypothesis.

# Overview
%%%
tag := "stone-overview"
%%%

As you will recall, a quantum state evolves as $`\psi(t) = e^{-it\hat H}\psi_0` (with
$`\hbar = 1`), and the Schrödinger equation $`i\,d\psi/dt = \hat H\psi` says that the
derivative of the evolution at $`t = 0` is $`-i\hat H`. Every continuous one-parameter symmetry
works the same way: translations along a direction are generated by a component of momentum,
rotations about an axis by a component of angular momentum. For a bounded
$`\hat H` the exponential is a norm-convergent power series. The Hamiltonians of physics are
usually unbounded, and then neither the series nor the derivative makes sense on all of $`H`.
Stone's theorem makes the correspondence exact.

A _one-parameter unitary group_ is a family of unitaries $`U(t)`, $`t \in \mathbb{R}`, with
$`U(0) = \mathbf{1}` and $`U(s + t) = U(s)U(t)`. It is _strongly continuous_ when
$`t \mapsto U(t)\psi` is continuous for every $`\psi`. Norm continuity would be too strong: the
translations $`(U(t)\psi)(x) = \psi(x + t)` on $`L^2(\mathbb{R})` are strongly but not norm
continuous. The _infinitesimal generator_ of $`U` is

$$`A\psi = \lim_{t \to 0,\ t \neq 0} \frac{1}{i}\,\frac{U(t)\psi - \psi}{t},`

defined on the $`\psi` for which the limit exists. With this sign, $`e^{itA}` has generator $`A`,
and the time evolution $`e^{-it\hat H}` has generator $`-\hat H`. Stone's theorem says that
$`U \mapsto A` and $`A \mapsto (t \mapsto e^{itA})` are mutually inverse bijections between the
strongly continuous one-parameter unitary groups and the self-adjoint operators on $`H`.

The section is formalised in these parts:

* _One-parameter unitary groups._ The group law and strong continuity; continuity at $`t = 0`
  suffices; $`U(t)^* = U(-t)`; and conjugation by a unitary $`W`: $`t \mapsto WU(t)W^{-1}` is again
  a one-parameter unitary group, with generator $`WAW^{-1}` on $`W\,\mathrm{Dom}(A)`.
* _The generator._ Its domain and value, both characterised by the limit above; the symmetry
  identity $`\langle \varphi, A\psi \rangle = \langle A\varphi, \psi \rangle`; and, for
  $`\psi \in \mathrm{Dom}(A)`, $`U(t)\psi \in \mathrm{Dom}(A)`, $`AU(t)\psi = U(t)A\psi`, and
  $`t \mapsto U(t)\psi` is differentiable with derivative $`iAU(t)\psi`. This last statement is
  the Schrödinger equation in the Hilbert-space sense.
* _The bounded case._ For bounded self-adjoint $`P`, the exponential series $`\exp(itP)` is a
  strongly continuous one-parameter unitary group with generator $`P`, defined on all of $`H`.
* _The exponential of a self-adjoint operator._ For self-adjoint $`A`,
  $`e^{itA} = \int e^{it\lambda} \, d\mu^A(\lambda)` is a strongly continuous one-parameter unitary
  group whose generator is $`A`, domains included.
* _Density._ The generator of a strongly continuous one-parameter unitary group is densely
  defined.
* _Stone's theorem._ The generator $`A` of a strongly continuous one-parameter unitary group
  $`U` is self-adjoint, $`U(t) = e^{itA}` for every $`t`, and $`A` is the only self-adjoint
  operator with this property.
* _Positive energy._ A family of unitaries has positive energy when it is a strongly continuous
  one-parameter unitary group whose generator, in general unbounded, is a positive operator. By
  Stone's theorem this holds exactly when $`V(t) = e^{itA}` with $`A` positive and self-adjoint.
  It is the form in which the library states the spectrum condition.
* _Weak continuity._ A family of unitaries $`U(x)`, indexed by any topological space, whose matrix
  elements $`\langle \xi, U(x)\eta \rangle` are continuous for $`\xi`, $`\eta` in a dense set, is
  strongly continuous.

Every statement of Section 10.4 is proved in Lean, as are the positive-energy results and the
weak-continuity lemma, with no `sorry`. The four standard results the blueprint states without
proof (the Bochner integral, smooth approximate identities, two facts about derivatives, and the
calculus of convolutions) are Mathlib theorems. Where a result assumes anything of $`H`, it is
completeness; no result assumes $`H` separable or nonzero. The declarations of Section 10.4 are
in the namespace `Physicslib4.Spectral.Stone`, positive energy is in `Physicslib4.AQFT`, and the
weak-continuity lemma is in `Physicslib4`.

# Design
%%%
tag := "stone-design"
%%%

Section 10.4 of the blueprint has no paragraph labelled "Formalization note"; its remarks on the
Lean formalization sit inside entries, mostly naming the Mathlib results a proof uses, and this
section links the entries it draws on. The conventions of the
{ref "unbounded-design"}[unbounded chapter] carry over: an unbounded operator is a partial linear
map `H →ₗ.[ℂ] H`, equality of operators includes equality of domains, symmetry assumes no density,
and self-adjointness is Mathlib's `IsSelfAdjoint`.

## Unitarity, the group law and continuity are separate

A unitary is a linear isometry equivalence `H ≃ₗᵢ[ℂ] H`, as everywhere in the library, so a
family of unitaries is a function `U : ℝ → (H ≃ₗᵢ[ℂ] H)` and is unitary by its type. The group law
is the structure `IsOneParameterUnitaryGroup U`, whose two fields state $`U(0)\psi = \psi` and
$`U(s + t)\psi = U(s)U(t)\psi` vector by vector. Strong continuity is the separate predicate
`IsStronglyContinuous U`, defined for any family. Each result assumes only what it uses: the
symmetry identity of the generator needs the group law but not continuity, and the weak-continuity
lemma needs neither.

## The generator is a partial map defined by a limit

`generator U` is a partial linear map. Its domain, `generatorDomain U`, is the submodule of the
$`\psi` for which the difference quotient `generatorQuotient U ψ t`, that is
$`(it)^{-1}(U(t)\psi - \psi)`, converges along the punctured filter `𝓝[≠] 0`; its value is
Mathlib's `limUnder` along that filter. The domain is exactly the set where the orbit has a
derivative at $`t = 0`, as in the blueprint
([def:hall-10.13](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#def:hall-10.13)).
{apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Stone.exists_generator_eq_iff}[`exists_generator_eq_iff`] handles
membership and value together: $`\psi` is in the domain with $`A\psi = \chi` exactly when the
quotient tends to $`\chi`. At $`t = 0` the quotient is $`0`, by Lean's convention $`0^{-1} = 0`,
and the punctured limit never uses this value.

The definition assumes nothing about `U`: no group law, no continuity, and no density of the
domain, which is a theorem rather than part of the type. Because the library's symmetry predicate
does not presuppose a dense domain, the symmetry identity is itself the statement that the
generator is symmetric,
{apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Stone.isSymmetric_generator}[`isSymmetric_generator`], for every
one-parameter unitary group (the remark in
[lmm:generator-symmetry-identity](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#lmm:generator-symmetry-identity)).

## The exponential comes from the functional calculus

For unbounded $`A` the series $`\sum_n (itA)^n/n!` is meaningless, since $`A^n\psi` need not be
defined. The library defines $`e^{itA}` as `expUnitary hA t`, the bounded integral of
$`f_t(\lambda) = e^{it\lambda}` (`expFun t`) against the spectral measure of $`A`, which is
unitary and is made an element of `H ≃ₗᵢ[ℂ] H`.
{apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Stone.functionalCalculus_expFun}[`functionalCalculus_expFun`]
identifies it with the functional calculus $`f_t(A)`, so the results on bounded integrals against a
projection-valued measure apply. The spectral measure and the functional calculus are described in
the {ref "unbounded"}[unbounded chapter]. The bounded case uses Mathlib's series `NormedSpace.exp`
instead, which needs no spectral theorem.

`expUnitary` takes the proof `hA : IsSelfAdjoint A` as an argument, since only a self-adjoint
operator has a spectral measure. For this reason Stone's theorem states the self-adjointness of
the generator as an existential, `∃ hA : IsSelfAdjoint (generator U), …`, and writes
$`e^{itA}` as `expUnitary hA t`.

Strong continuity of $`e^{itA}` and its derivative at $`t = 0` come from norm identities:
$`\|e^{itA}\psi - e^{isA}\psi\|^2 = \int |e^{it\lambda} - e^{is\lambda}|^2 \, d\mu^A_\psi(\lambda)`,
and for $`\psi \in \mathrm{Dom}(A)` the squared error of the difference quotient is
$`\int |g_t(\lambda) - \lambda|^2 \, d\mu^A_\psi(\lambda)`, with
$`g_t(\lambda) = (e^{it\lambda} - 1)/(it)`. Both tend to $`0` by dominated convergence in Mathlib's
filter form, `tendsto_integral_filter_of_dominated_convergence` (the proofs of
[lmm:exp-strongly-continuous](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#lmm:exp-strongly-continuous) and
[lmm:exp-derivative-at-zero](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#lmm:exp-derivative-at-zero)), along $`s \to t` and along the
punctured filter at $`0`, with dominating functions $`4` and $`4\lambda^2`; the second is
integrable because $`\psi \in \mathrm{Dom}(A)`. So the generator of $`e^{itA}` extends $`A`. It is
symmetric, and a self-adjoint operator has no proper symmetric extension, so it equals $`A`
([prpstn:hall-10.14](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#prpstn:hall-10.14)).

## Density by smoothing

For a real weight $`f`, the average $`B_f\psi = \int f(\tau)\,U(\tau)\psi \, d\tau` is
`average U f ψ`, a Bochner integral. The density proof
([lmm:hall-10.18](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#lmm:hall-10.18))
writes it as a convolution, $`U(t)B_f\psi = (f \star g_\psi)(-t)` with $`g_\psi(x) = U(-x)\psi`
({apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Stone.average_eq_convolution}[`average_eq_convolution`]), so that
Mathlib's convolution calculus does the analysis.
`HasCompactSupport.hasDerivAt_convolution_left` differentiates $`t \mapsto U(t)B_f\psi` for a
compactly supported $`C^1` weight, which puts $`B_f\psi` in the domain of the generator, and
`ContDiffBump.convolution_tendsto_right_of_continuous` gives $`B_{f_n}\psi \to \psi` for normalised
bump functions of outer radius $`1/(n + 1)`. The density theorem assumes $`H` complete: without
completeness, Mathlib's Bochner integral is $`0`. The argument is the one-parameter case of
Gårding's smoothing argument.

## The proof of the theorem, and uniqueness

The proof of {apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Stone.stone}[`stone`] follows the blueprint
([thrm:hall-10.15](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#thrm:hall-10.15)).
The generator $`A` is essentially self-adjoint, because the deficiency subspaces
$`\mathrm{Ker}(A^* \mp i\mathbf{1})` are trivial: for $`\psi` in one of them and
$`\varphi \in \mathrm{Dom}(A)`, $`\langle U(t)\varphi, \psi \rangle` is a bounded solution of
$`y' = \pm y`, hence $`0`. The orbits of $`U` and of $`e^{itA^{(\mathrm{cl})}}`, for the closure
$`A^{(\mathrm{cl})}`, agree on $`\mathrm{Dom}(A)`, since their difference starts at $`0` and has
constant norm, so $`U(t) = e^{itA^{(\mathrm{cl})}}` by density. The generator of
$`e^{itA^{(\mathrm{cl})}}` is $`A^{(\mathrm{cl})}`, so $`A = A^{(\mathrm{cl})}`.

Uniqueness needs no further argument. The generator is a function of `U`, so if
$`U(t) = e^{itB}` for a self-adjoint $`B`, the generator of $`U` is the generator of $`e^{itB}`,
which is $`B`. The same argument gives
{apiDocs Physicslib4.AQFT.generator_eq_of_eq_expUnitary}[`generator_eq_of_eq_expUnitary`], for
any family $`V` with $`V(t) = e^{itB}`. For bounded operators,
{apiDocs Physicslib4.AQFT.exp_generator_unique}[`exp_generator_unique`] proves uniqueness directly,
by differentiating $`\exp(itP)` at $`t = 0`.

## Positive energy is a property of the generator

{apiDocs Physicslib4.AQFT.IsPositiveEnergy}[`IsPositiveEnergy V`] is a structure with three
fields: the group law, strong continuity, and positivity of `generator V`, which is the library's
`Spectral.Unbounded.IsPositive` (symmetric, with $`\mathrm{Re}\langle \psi, A\psi \rangle \ge 0`
on the domain). The condition is imposed on the generator itself, which may be unbounded, and
needs no self-adjointness hypothesis, since the generator is defined for every family. Stone's
theorem then gives the textbook form
([thrm:positive-energy-iff-exp](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#thrm:positive-energy-iff-exp)):
$`V` has positive energy exactly when $`V(t) = e^{itA}` with $`A` positive and self-adjoint.
For time translations the unitaries are $`U(t) = e^{it\hat H}`, in the convention
$`U(a) = e^{iP\cdot a}`, the inverse of the Schrödinger evolution, so their generator is
$`\hat H` and positive energy says $`\hat H \ge 0`.

The definition refers to no spacetime. A vacuum state
({apiDocs Physicslib4.AQFT.HaagKastler.HaagKastlerNet.IsVacuumState}[`HaagKastlerNet.IsVacuumState`])
asks for positive energy of the unitary group of every future-timelike translation subgroup, and a
ground state for a flow, in Minkowski or curved spacetime, asks it of the unitary group
implementing the flow; see the {ref "haag-kastler"}[Haag–Kastler chapter] and the
{ref "curved"}[curved-spacetime chapter].

## Where weak continuity suffices

Stone's theorem assumes strong continuity, and for a one-parameter unitary group continuity at
$`t = 0` suffices
({apiDocs Physicslib4.Spectral.Stone.isStronglyContinuous_iff_tendsto_zero}[`isStronglyContinuous_iff_tendsto_zero`]).
In the applications the unitaries come from the GNS construction of an invariant state $`\omega`,
where what can be computed are the matrix elements
$`\langle \pi(a)\Omega, U(g)\pi(b)\Omega \rangle = \omega(a^*\gamma_g(b))` on the dense set of
vectors $`\pi(a)\Omega`.
{apiDocs Physicslib4.strongContinuous_of_weak}[`strongContinuous_of_weak`] upgrades their
continuity to strong continuity. It is stated for any family of unitaries indexed by a topological
space, with no group law and no completeness of $`H`, and taking the dense set to be all of $`H`
shows that a weakly continuous family of unitaries is strongly continuous. The GNS theorems
{apiDocs Physicslib4.GNS.exists_gns_unitary_of_invariant_strongContinuous}[`exists_gns_unitary_of_invariant_strongContinuous`]
and its bundled form use it
([thrm:invariant-state-gns-unitary-general](https://physicslib.github.io/physicslib4/blueprint/chptr-haag-kastler-axioms-blueprint.html#thrm:invariant-state-gns-unitary-general);
see also the {ref "gns-design"}[GNS chapter]).

